/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A fixed-length code wastes bits when one symbol dominates.
   - The Kraft inequality says that for any binary prefix code, Σ 2−lᵢ ≤ 1, where lᵢ is the length in bits of the codeword for symbol i.
   - L̄ is the average codeword length in bits per symbol; pᵢ is the probability of symbol i; lᵢ is the length in bits of its codeword.
   - H(X) is the source entropy in bits per symbol.
   - η is the coding efficiency, a pure ratio; H(X) and L̄ must use the same logarithm base, normally base 2.
   - Shannon-Fano builds a code top-down by repeated splitting.
   - The split is a heuristic: sort first, and where two cuts tie, try both.
   - Huffman builds a code bottom-up by merging the least likely symbols first.
   - The two least likely symbols become siblings at the greatest depth.
   - Worked example 2 shows a source where Shannon-Fano loses to Huffman by 0.01 bit/symbol.
*/
