/* book Worked example 2: Q: A source emits A, B, C, D, E with probabilities 0.35, 0.17, 0.17, 0.16 and 0.15. Build the Shannon-Fano code, find L̄ and η, and compare with the Huffman code of the same source.
   1. Entropy: H = 0.35 log₂(1/0.35) + 2 × 0.17 log₂(1/0.17) + 0.16 log₂(1/0.16) + 0.15 log₂(1/0.15) = 0.5301 + 0.8692 + 0.4230 + 0.4105 = 2.2328 bits/symbol.
   2. The list is already sorted. Try each cut of the full list (first three rows of the table) and keep the one with the smallest difference: {A, B} | {C, D, E} gives 0.52 against 0.48.
   3. Split {A, B}: A = 00, B = 01. Split {C, D, E}: {C} | {D, E} gives 0.17 against 0.31 (difference 0.14), better than {C, D} | {E} (0.33 against 0.15, difference 0.18). So C = 10.
   4. Split {D, E}: D = 110, E = 111. Lengths 2, 2, 2, 3, 3.
   5. L̄ = 2(0.35 + 0.17 + 0.17) + 3(0.16 + 0.15) = 1.38 + 0.93 = 2.31 bits/symbol; η = 2.2328/2.31 = 96.66 %.
   6. Huffman: merge E + D = 0.31, then B + C = 0.34, then 0.31 + 0.34 = 0.65, then A + 0.65 = 1. Lengths 1, 3, 3, 3, 3, so L̄ = 0.35 + 3(0.65) = 2.30 bits/symbol and η = 97.08 %.
   7. Check Kraft for both: 3(1/4) + 2(1/8) = 1 and 1/2 + 4(1/8) = 1.
   Answer:  */
