/* book exam traps: Splitting an unsorted Shannon-Fano list. The rule assumes decreasing order; a valid but poor code can result. | Reporting one symbol's codeword length as the average length. L̄ is the probability-weighted sum Σ pᵢ lᵢ, not a single lᵢ. | Mixing natural logs with base-2 lengths. Use base 2 for both; answers are in bits per symbol. | Writing redundancy as H(X)/L̄. Efficiency is H(X)/L̄; redundancy is 1 − H(X)/L̄. | Claiming the Huffman code of a source is unique. Tie choices change the codewords and the variance of the lengths, never L̄. */
