/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take two discrete random variables X and Y.
   - P(xi,yj) is the probability that X=xi and Y=yj; the sum runs over all pairs; entropy is in bits when log₂ is used.
   - Conditional entropy H(X|Y) is the uncertainty left in X after Y is known:
   - P(xi|yj) is the conditional probability of xi given yj.
   - Both formulas use the convention 0 log₂ 0 = 0, so terms with zero probability contribute nothing to the sum.
   - The chain rule splits the joint uncertainty:
   - H(X) is the source entropy from Chapter 7.1.
   - Mutual information is the reduction in uncertainty:
   - I(X;Y) is mutual information in bits, H(X) and H(Y) are source and output entropies, and H(X|Y), H(Y|X) are conditional entropies.
   - I(X;Y) is symmetric: I(X;Y)=I(Y;X).
*/
