/* book Worked example 2: Q: The same BSC (p = 0.1) is driven with P(X = 0) = 0.7 and P(X = 1) = 0.3. Find P(Y = 0), H(Y), I(X;Y) and H(X|Y), and compare I with worked example 1.
   1. Output probability: P(Y = 0) = 0.7 × 0.9 + 0.3 × 0.1 = 0.63 + 0.03 = 0.66, so P(Y = 1) = 0.34.
   2. H(Y) = H(0.66) = 0.66 log₂(1/0.66) + 0.34 log₂(1/0.34) = 0.3956 + 0.5292 = 0.9248 bits.
   3. H(Y|X) = H(0.1) = 0.469 bits, whatever the input probabilities, because each row of the channel matrix is the same pair 0.9, 0.1.
   4. I(X;Y) = H(Y) − H(Y|X) = 0.9248 − 0.4690 = 0.4558 bits/symbol.
   5. Check with the other form: H(X) = H(0.7) = 0.8813 bits; the joint table gives H(X,Y) = 1.3503 bits, so H(X|Y) = H(X,Y) − H(Y) = 1.3503 − 0.9248 = 0.4255 bits and H(X) − H(X|Y) = 0.8813 − 0.4255 = 0.4558 bits. The two forms agree.
   6. Comparison: 0.4558 < 0.531 bits. The unequal input wastes part of the channel; the equal input of worked example 1 is the best one, which is why chapter 7.4 calls 0.531 bits the capacity.
   Answer:  */
