/* book exam traps: Swapping H(X|Y) and H(Y|X). They answer different questions and are not generally equal; only symmetric cases make them equal. | Claiming I(X;Y) can be larger than H(X) or H(Y). The overlap cannot be larger than either region; 0 ≤ I ≤ min(H(X),H(Y)). | Writing I = H(X) + H(Y) + H(X,Y). H(X) + H(Y) counts the overlap twice; subtracting H(X,Y), which counts it once, leaves the overlap alone. With the plus sign the result is larger than H(X,Y) itself, so it cannot be a shared part. | Reading a channel matrix row as an output distribution that must sum with other rows. Each row is conditional on one input and sums to 1; columns need not sum to 1. | For a BSC, plugging p into I directly. The input-dependent term is H(Y); the fixed noise term is H(p). */
